[For "white"]
[Kind "trap"]
[Start "sicilian-nc6-d6 5.Nf3"]
[Name "...d6 and ...Bg4: the queen for a piece"]

5... Bg4 { The pin, before the knight has come to f6. [%icon pin@f3] }
6. d5 { Push past the knight, as before. It cannot stay on c6, and the pinned knight on f3 is about to show why that matters. [%cal Gd5c6] }
6... Bxf3 { The safe way out: Black gives up the bishop. }
( 6... Ne5?? { The most common move, and it loses at once. }
  7. Nxe5 { The pinned knight moves anyway. If Black takes it, Qxg4 wins the bishop, because nothing on f6 guards g4. }
  7... Bxd1 8. Bb5+ { Black's queen is the only piece that can block. [%cal Gb5e8] }
  8... Qd7 9. Bxd7+ { The queen is won back. After the king moves, Kxd1 takes the bishop too, and White is two pieces ahead. } )
7. gxf3 { The pawn takes back. It looks ugly, but White gets the two bishops, and the pawn on f3 covers e4. [%cal Gf3e4] }
7... Ne5
8. f4 { The knight has to move again. }
8... Nd7
9. Nc3 { White has the two bishops, a big centre and an easy lead in development: the engine has White more than two pawns ahead. } *
